<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 7 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Americas before Columbus</h1><p class="path-tagline">Great cities, empires and roads built long before 1492.</p><p class="path-intro">When Europeans arrived, the Americas already held some of the largest cities on earth, roads that outran anything in Europe, and empires that had risen and fallen for two thousand years. This path crosses that world: the metropolis of Teotihuacan, the island capital of the Aztecs, and the Inca realm that ran ten thousand kilometres of mountain road - and the two emperors who met the conquistadors.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Builder</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/teotihuacan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 100 BCE – 550 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Teotihuacan</span><span class="path-step-why">A city of a hundred thousand people, planned on a grid, that shaped Mesoamerica centuries before the Aztecs.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/aztec-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · 1325 CE – 1521 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Aztec Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The last and greatest power of Mesoamerica: tribute, war and a capital on a lake.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Tenochtitlan</span><span class="path-step-why">The island metropolis that stunned the Spaniards, larger than most cities of Europe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1466–1520</span><span class="path-step-name">Moctezuma II</span><span class="path-step-why">The emperor who received Cortes, and whose fate still divides historians.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/inca-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1438 CE – 1533 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Inca Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">The largest state of the pre-Columbian Americas, run by roads, storehouses and knotted cords instead of writing.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/machu-picchu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machu-picchu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Place</span><span class="path-step-name">Machu Picchu</span><span class="path-step-why">The royal estate in the clouds that conquest never found.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/atahualpa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1502–1533</span><span class="path-step-name">Atahualpa</span><span class="path-step-why">The Inca who won a civil war just in time to meet Pizarro at Cajamarca.</span></span></a></li></ol>
